<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Multiple Style Capability Experiment</h2>

<figure id="A1.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.07430v1/template_demo.png" id="A1.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/296;" width="548" height="296" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>On the left, we construct a base model that outputs multiple solution styles for SQL problems. This model is then used for reinforcement learning training. We calculated the probability of the number of times the model outputted different styles across 32 samples.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We construct a base model that outputs five different solution styles for SQL problems. This model is then used for reinforcement learning training.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we sample each question 32 times. Based on the output prefix, we count the number of times each distinct style appears within these 32 samples. For prefixes that are not among our original five styles (e.g., the model combines several different prefixes), we categorize them as ”Others” and treat them as an additional style.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The result shows in Figure <a href="#A1.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Appendix A Multiple Style Capability Experiment ‣ The Choice of Divergence: A Neglected Key to Mitigating Diversity Collapse in Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Reward" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. On the base model, we find that it can fully output all styles. However, after training with reverse KL-constrained GRPO, most outputs degenerate into a single style. In contrast, forward KL significantly mitigates the degradation of the model’s ability to produce different styles.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>RLVR Algorithms</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO)</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">GRPO presents an innovative approach to policy learning that distinguishes itself from methods like Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) by eliminating the need for an explicit value function. Instead, GRPO computes the advantage in a group-relative manner, offering a streamlined yet effective optimization strategy.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For a specific question-answer pair <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(q,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(q,a)</annotation></semantics></math>, GRPO’s underlying behavior policy, <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>old</mtext></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, generates a group of <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> individual responses, denoted as <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{o_{i}\}_{i=1}^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{o_{i}\}_{i=1}^{G}</annotation></semantics></math>. The advantage for the <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th response within this ensemble is then precisely calculated by normalizing the rewards specific to that group, <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{R_{i}\}_{i=1}^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{R_{i}\}_{i=1}^{G}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A2.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}_{i,t}=\frac{r_{i}-\text{mean}(\{R_{i}\}_{i=1}^{G})}{\text{std}(\{R_{i}\}_{i=1}^{G})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mtext>mean</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mtext>std</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}_{i,t}=\frac{r_{i}-\text{mean}(\{R_{i}\}_{i=1}^{G})}{\text{std}(\{R_{i}\}_{i=1}^{G})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Similar to the clipped surrogate objective found in PPO, GRPO also incorporates a clipping mechanism to constrain policy updates. This helps maintain training stability and improve sample efficiency by ensuring that new policies don’t deviate too drastically from previous ones. Beyond this, GRPO further enhances regularization by directly adding a Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence penalty term to its objective function. This penalty helps prevent the policy from drifting too far from a reference policy, promoting stable and controlled learning.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">The comprehensive objective function for GRPO is articulated as:</p>
<table id="A2.E13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A2.E13X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E13X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}\text{GRPO}(\theta)=-\mathbb{E}_{\begin{subarray}{c}(q,a)\sim\mathcal{D}\\
{o_{i}}\sim\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}(\cdot\mid q)\end{subarray}}\Bigg[" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>GRPO</mtext><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>o</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>old</mtext></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></msub><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">[</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}\text{GRPO}(\theta)=-\mathbb{E}_{\begin{subarray}{c}(q,a)\sim\mathcal{D}\\
{o_{i}}\sim\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}(\cdot\mid q)\end{subarray}}\Bigg[</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E13X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{1}{G}\sum_{i=1}^{G}\frac{1}{|o_{i}|}\sum_{t=1}^{|o_{i}|}\Bigg(" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>G</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">(</mo><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{1}{G}\sum_{i=1}^{G}\frac{1}{|o_{i}|}\sum_{t=1}^{|o_{i}|}\Bigg(</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="3" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.E13Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E13Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min\left(\rho_{i,t}(\theta)\hat{A}_{i,t},\ \text{clip}\left(\rho_{i,t}(\theta),1-\varepsilon,1+\varepsilon\right)\hat{A}_{i,t}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mtext>clip</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min\left(\rho_{i,t}(\theta)\hat{A}_{i,t},\ \text{clip}\left(\rho_{i,t}(\theta),1-\varepsilon,1+\varepsilon\right)\hat{A}_{i,t}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="A2.E13Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E13Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle-\eta D_{\text{KL}}(\pi_{\theta}||\pi_{\text{ref}})\Bigg)\Bigg]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>η</mi><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">)</mo><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-\eta D_{\text{KL}}(\pi_{\theta}||\pi_{\text{ref}})\Bigg)\Bigg],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is a coefficient for the KL penalty, <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\text{KL}}(\pi_{\theta}||\pi_{\text{ref}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\text{KL}}(\pi_{\theta}||\pi_{\text{ref}})</annotation></semantics></math> quantifies the KL divergence between the current policy <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> and a specified reference policy <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math>. The term <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{i,t}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{i,t}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> represents the importance sampling ratio for the <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th response at time <math id="A2.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, which is defined as:</p>
<table id="A2.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{i,t}(\theta)=\frac{\pi_{\theta}(o_{i,t}\mid q,o_{i,&lt;t})}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}(o_{i,t}\mid q,o_{i,&lt;t})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>old</mtext></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{i,t}(\theta)=\frac{\pi_{\theta}(o_{i,t}\mid q,o_{i,&lt;t})}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}(o_{i,t}\mid q,o_{i,&lt;t})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">A critical design choice in GRPO is its sample-level objective computation. Specifically, the loss is first averaged within each generated sequence, and subsequently, these sequence-level losses are averaged across various samples. This distinct computational approach, particularly when compared to token-level optimizations, can significantly influence the algorithm’s empirical performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Traditional GRPO applies a single, unified loss function to all training samples. This loss includes a KL divergence penalty, typically in the form of reverse KL divergence, which aims to keep the fine-tuned policy close to the base policy. This penalty is added directly to the policy gradient loss. When applied to our experimental setup, the KL penalty for samples from <math id="A2.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{exp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>exp</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{exp}}</annotation></semantics></math> would be:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A2.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E15.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{KL-GRPO}}=\mathbb{E}_{q\sim\mathcal{D}_{\text{exp}}}\left[-\beta D_{\text{KL}}(\pi_{\theta}||\pi_{\text{base}})\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>KL-GRPO</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>exp</mtext></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{KL-GRPO}}=\mathbb{E}_{q\sim\mathcal{D}_{\text{exp}}}\left[-\beta D_{\text{KL}}(\pi_{\theta}||\pi_{\text{base}})\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">where the reverse KL divergence is defined as:</p>
<table id="A2.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\text{KL}}(\pi_{\theta}||\pi_{\text{base}})=\sum_{x}\pi_{\theta}(x)\log\left(\frac{\pi_{\theta}(x)}{\pi_{\text{base}}(x)}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>x</mi></munder><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>log</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\text{KL}}(\pi_{\theta}||\pi_{\text{base}})=\sum_{x}\pi_{\theta}(x)\log\left(\frac{\pi_{\theta}(x)}{\pi_{\text{base}}(x)}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Here, the term <math id="A2.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\text{KL}}(\pi_{\theta}||\pi_{\text{base}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\text{KL}}(\pi_{\theta}||\pi_{\text{base}})</annotation></semantics></math> represents the <span id="A2.SS1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reverse KL Divergence</span>. It measures the information lost when the base policy <math id="A2.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{base}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{base}}</annotation></semantics></math> approximates the new policy <math id="A2.SS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>. This choice heavily penalizes the new policy <math id="A2.SS1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> for exploring actions that the base policy <math id="A2.SS1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{base}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{base}}</annotation></semantics></math> considers to have a low probability. In other words, if the new policy <math id="A2.SS1.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> assigns a high probability to an action where the base policy <math id="A2.SS1.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{base}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{base}}</annotation></semantics></math> assigns a near-zero probability, this divergence will be very large. The effect is to strongly encourage the new policy <math id="A2.SS1.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> to stick to the modes (high-probability regions) of the original base policy <math id="A2.SS1.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{base}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{base}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which restricts the exploration space.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Dynamic Sampling Policy Optimization (DAPO) </h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">DAPO is an enhancement of the GRPO algorithm, incorporating several key improvements. DAPO eliminates the KL penalty and refines the clipping mechanism, changing the upper bound from <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1+\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1+\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> to a fixed value of <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1+\varepsilon_{\text{upper}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mtext>upper</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1+\varepsilon_{\text{upper}})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{\text{upper}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mtext>upper</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{\text{upper}}</annotation></semantics></math> is set to 0.28. A core innovation of DAPO is its dynamic sampling mechanism, which moves beyond the ”all-or-nothing” approach to sampling. Additionally, the algorithm applies a token-level policy gradient loss and uses an overlong reward shaping technique.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Method for Divergence Definition</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For f-divergences like forward KL, the Divergence Definition and Generator implementations are equivalent. However, for divergences such as JS divergence, which require sampling from both the reference policy <math id="A3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{ref}</annotation></semantics></math> and the new policy <math id="A3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A3.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E17.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\text{JS}(\pi_{\text{base}}||\pi_{\theta})=\frac{1}{2}D_{\text{KL}}\left(\pi_{\text{base}}||\frac{\pi_{\text{base}}+\pi_{\theta}}{2}\right)+\frac{1}{2}D_{\text{KL}}\left(\pi_{\theta}||\frac{\pi_{\text{base}}+\pi_{\theta}}{2}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>JS</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{JS}(\pi_{\text{base}}||\pi_{\theta})=\frac{1}{2}D_{\text{KL}}\left(\pi_{\text{base}}||\frac{\pi_{\text{base}}+\pi_{\theta}}{2}\right)+\frac{1}{2}D_{\text{KL}}\left(\pi_{\theta}||\frac{\pi_{\text{base}}+\pi_{\theta}}{2}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since the JS divergence is composed of two parts, we use data from the <math id="A3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{pef}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>pef</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{pef}}</annotation></semantics></math> loss to compute the first part <math id="A3.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\text{KL}}\left(\pi_{\text{base}}||\frac{\pi_{\text{base}}+\pi_{\theta}}{2}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\text{KL}}\left(\pi_{\text{base}}||\frac{\pi_{\text{base}}+\pi_{\theta}}{2}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. For the training on the <math id="A3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{exp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>exp</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{exp}}</annotation></semantics></math> loss, we introduce a reference model to calculate the value of the second part <math id="A3.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\text{KL}}\left(\pi_{\theta}||\frac{\pi_{\text{base}}+\pi_{\theta}}{2}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\text{KL}}\left(\pi_{\theta}||\frac{\pi_{\text{base}}+\pi_{\theta}}{2}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The loss for DPH-JS on the <math id="A3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{exp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>exp</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{exp}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be written as:</p>
<table id="A3.E18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A3.E18X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E18X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPH-exp}}(\theta)=-\mathbb{E}_{\begin{subarray}{c}(q,a)\sim\mathcal{D}_{\text{exp}}\\
o_{i}\sim\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}(\cdot\mid q)\end{subarray}}\Bigg[" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>DPH-exp</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>exp</mtext></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>o</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>old</mtext></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></msub><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">[</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPH-exp}}(\theta)=-\mathbb{E}_{\begin{subarray}{c}(q,a)\sim\mathcal{D}_{\text{exp}}\\
o_{i}\sim\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}(\cdot\mid q)\end{subarray}}\Bigg[</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E18X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{1}{G}\sum_{i=1}^{G}\frac{1}{|o_{i}|}\sum_{t=1}^{|o_{i}|}\min\left(\rho_{i,t}(\theta)\hat{A}_{i,t},\ \text{clip}\left(\rho_{i,t}(\theta),1-\varepsilon,1+\varepsilon\right)\hat{A}_{i,t}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>G</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mtext>clip</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{1}{G}\sum_{i=1}^{G}\frac{1}{|o_{i}|}\sum_{t=1}^{|o_{i}|}\min\left(\rho_{i,t}(\theta)\hat{A}_{i,t},\ \text{clip}\left(\rho_{i,t}(\theta),1-\varepsilon,1+\varepsilon\right)\hat{A}_{i,t}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A3.E18Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E18Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle-\beta_{1}D_{\text{KL}}\left(\pi_{\theta}||\frac{\pi_{\text{base}}+\pi_{\theta}}{2}\right)\Bigg]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-\beta_{1}D_{\text{KL}}\left(\pi_{\theta}||\frac{\pi_{\text{base}}+\pi_{\theta}}{2}\right)\Bigg]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A3.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1}=\eta*\frac{|\mathcal{D}_{\text{pef}}|}{|\mathcal{D}_{\text{exp}}|}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>pef</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>exp</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1}=\eta*\frac{|\mathcal{D}_{\text{pef}}|}{|\mathcal{D}_{\text{exp}}|}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">By adjusting the value of <math id="A3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can make the values of the two JS components within the same batch as close as possible, thereby achieving balance during training. <math id="A3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is not a hyperparameter; it only depends on the dataset size and <math id="A3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>. Its formula is:</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The loss for DPH-JS on the <math id="A3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{pef}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>pef</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{pef}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be expressed as:</p>
<table id="A3.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E20.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPH-pef}}=\mathbb{E}_{q\sim\mathcal{D}_{\text{pef}}}\left[D_{\text{KL}}\left(\pi_{\text{base}}||\frac{\pi_{\text{base}}+\pi_{\theta}}{2}\right)\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DPH-pef</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>pef</mtext></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>base</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPH-pef}}=\mathbb{E}_{q\sim\mathcal{D}_{\text{pef}}}\left[D_{\text{KL}}\left(\pi_{\text{base}}||\frac{\pi_{\text{base}}+\pi_{\theta}}{2}\right)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Training Details</h2>

<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>Task Settings</h3>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the SQL task, we used the Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct and OmniSQL32B models. We performed RL training exclusively on the BIRD dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#biba.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2024d)</a></cite> and then evaluated the models on both the BIRD and Spider <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#biba.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al. (2018)</a></cite> datasets. To validate the generalization of our method, we also tested it on a mathematical reasoning task, treating it as an out-of-distribution (OOD) evaluation.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the mathematical reasoning task, we used the Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct and Qwen2.5-Math-7B models. We conducted RL training on the DAPO-17K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#biba.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al. (2025)</a></cite> dataset and performed testing on seven different datasets: AIME24 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#biba.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2024a)</a></cite>, AIME25, AMC23 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#biba.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2024a)</a></cite>, Math500 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#biba.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al. (2021)</a></cite>, Olympiad <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#biba.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al. (2024)</a></cite>, Minerva <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#biba.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lewkowycz et al. (2022)</a></cite>, and College Math <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#biba.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tang et al. (2024)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation</h5>

<div id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the SQL tasks, the output is typically a tuple, such as <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[(\text{string},\text{int}),(\text{string},\text{int})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>string</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>int</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>string</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>int</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[(\text{string},\text{int}),(\text{string},\text{int})]</annotation></semantics></math>. We compute the Cartesian product of the prediction and the ground truth. For the math tasks, we use the official Qwen2.5 evaluation tool<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
                
                
                
              <a href="https://github.com/QwenLM/Qwen2.5-Math" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/QwenLM/Qwen2.5-Math</a></span></span></span> for a detailed assessment, as this tool provides tailored evaluations for each dataset.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2 </span>Method Settings</h3>

